<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Devil's Island</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Île du Diable</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Devil's Island is the smallest of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iles-du-salut">Îles du Salut</a> off the coast of French Guiana. From 1852 to 1953 it formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s penal colony and was used especially for political prisoners, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a>, held there from 1895 to 1899. Its harsh conditions made the name a byword for brutal imprisonment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/devils-island.jpg" alt="Devil's Island" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arria Belli · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tour_dreyfus_kourou.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
